"An object diagram illustrates a concrete configuration of instances and links at a stated moment.\n\nUse when: Test whether a proposed class model supports an example. Explain a surprising instance relationship with representative values.\n\nAvoid when: The reader needs all possible states or the complete type model.\n\nVisual grammar: Underlined instance labels identify objects and their classifiers. Slots show relevant values without exposing sensitive records. Links represent instance-level relationships rather than type dependencies.\n\nBuild: Choose one scenario and mark its snapshot moment. Create minimal representative objects from the class model. Populate the slots needed to explain the example. Arrange linked objects with padded compartments and route links away from values.\n\nCheck: Each instance conforms to its classifier and multiplicities. Identity is distinguished from equal attribute values. Illustrative values are labeled synthetic when appropriate. Links terminate at objects without covering slot text.\n\nPitfalls: Presenting hypothetical sample values as observed production data. Using type associations where actual instance links are intended.\n\nExample: Order o17 has two distinct OrderLine instances that reference the same Product, showing shared identity.\n\nAlternatives: Class diagram, Heap object graph."
